## Session 23 summary

**Gate status: 17 PASS / 1 FAIL.** `scaling_curve` is the open blocker (and `cache_coherency` is a low-rate flake to re-verify before the final gate).

### Infra fixed this session
- **All 16 VMs had stale QEMU LUN passthrough fds** after session 22's iSCSI relogin on clyde (VMs get `/dev/sda` as block-LUN passthrough of clyde's `/dev/disk/by-path/...disk1b-lun-0`; the relogout destroyed the host device under running QEMUs → every SCSI command aborted "I/O process terminated"). Fixed by `virsh destroy+start` of all 16 VMs. **Any future iSCSI relogin on clyde requires restarting all test VMs.**
- Re-exported `/src/mxfs` from clyde (`exportfs ... ,fsid=77 192.168.120.0/24:/src/mxfs`) — but note the criteria harness doesn't need it; `lib.sh` mounts `192.168.1.4:/src` directly in the VMs.
- `fresh_cluster_mount` fails silently when invoked from the interactive session shell (snapshot/`set -u` artifact) — **always wrap criterion/lib.sh invocations in `bash -c '...'`**.

### scaling_curve findings (RULE 4, ftrace-proven)
- The earlier "PASS ratio=100%" was an artifact of a cold-boot-slow 1-node baseline. Real state: warm 1n≈3.2-3.4s, any ≥2-node parallel rsync per-node wall ≈5.7-7s (ratio ~200%, gate ≤150%). It's a ~2.5s constant multi-node penalty on the slowest node, not super-linear.
- **Fix 1 (landed, verified)**: per-mkdir shortform-parent durability barrier (`mxfs_dlm_dir_inode_durable` in `xfs_create`) cost 1.2-1.6s/node (706 calls × ~1.8ms sync log force). Added `i_mxfs_self_created` flag (set in `xfs_icreate` pre-publish, cleared on any peer BAST in `mxfs_dlm_bast_notify`, false for inodes igot from disk) gating the barrier. Verified: 706→1 call. Files: `xfs/xfs_inode.h`, `xfs/xfs_inode.c`, `xfs/xfs_mxfs_dlm.c`.
- **Fix 2 (landed, net-neutral so far)**: flipped `mxfs_lazy_ag_drain` default 0→1 (eager sync AG drains were ~190 drains + ~400 sync log forces/node with zero contention — 0 ACQ-FRESH, AG grants never move thanks to existing node-affine dir+file allocation). Walls unchanged; keep but the remaining gap is elsewhere.
- **Disproven**: slow-path inode DLM acquires during rsync (new `P23-SLOWPATH` probe in `ilock_begin` fired 0× — the earlier `/proc/pid/stack` samples of running tasks were stale frames; only D-state samples are trustworthy).
- Current build `9BE476B617054ECC48E61D4` (includes both fixes + P23 probe). Max-node wall still ~5.7s; remaining suspects: per-rename/dir-open `mxfs_dir_drain_evict_data_blocks` log forces, `mxfs_dlm_dir_durable_signal` publish-before-notify in create/rename, and ~1900 `xfs_log_force` calls of unattributed context.

### Next step (ready to run)
`bash -c 'scripts/diag_vnop_profile.sh test1 test2'` — new in-tree profiler of the `xfs_vn_*` syscall entry points (rsync-context-only, no strace overhead), then the same solo (`scripts/diag_vnop_profile.sh test1`) to attribute the residual ~2.5s gap to a syscall class; drill into that op's mxfs hooks next. After scaling_curve goes green at 16 nodes on a **warm** baseline, re-run `cache_coherency` ~3× (rename_visibility/unlink_visibility flaked 1-of-3 today: node3's first two renames durably lost — lost-update family, unreproduced since), then `posix_semantics.sh --nodes 16` (never recorded), then full `verify_ship.sh`.

